Lot 4 — ncursesw design contract
--------------------------------

[x] Audit the existing process/spawn terminal lifecycle before designing curses:
    `process_terminal_internal.cpp` already owns terminal identity, bounded
    reservation, foreground PGID, termios snapshots, stale-monitor protection,
    stop/resume and asynchronous child-exit reclaim.
[x] Write and review `NCURSES_DESIGN.md` as the detailed Lot 5 implementation
    contract; keep ncurses decisions out of the fundamental product-contract
    section.
[x] Target statically linked `ncursesw` wide-character APIs for UTF-8; do not
    add a runtime `libncursesw.so` dependency or a supported ON/OFF CMake matrix.
[x] Preserve one interactive-terminal owner at a time with the explicit states
    normal, curses, child_process and terminal_reclaimed_curses_pending.
[x] Reuse/extend the existing terminal handoff registry rather than introducing
    a second terminal manager. Reservation remains a transient coordination
    mechanism, not another owner.
[x] Keep every ncurses call on the main thread. Extract/share Babet's existing
    main-thread identity only as needed by signal/curses/terminal handoff.
[x] Define one central main-thread terminal-event service helper. The existing
    monitor may reclaim PGID/termios and mark curses restoration pending but may
    never call reset_prog_mode(), doupdate() or any ncurses function.
[x] Define main-thread interactive spawn during curses as atomic suspension and
    handoff: save program mode -> endwin -> child foreground -> reclaim ->
    pending restore -> main-thread reset/redraw. Curses calls never steal the
    terminal from a still-running child.
[x] Reject worker operations that would take the interactive terminal while
    curses is active; preserve worker non-interactive spawn. Include foreground
    process resume in the same rule.
[x] Define explicit Babet-owned signal policy: SIGWINCH is a coalesced symbolic
    curses resize event; SIGTSTP/SIGCONT use controlled main-thread suspend and
    resume; SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGHUP preserve logical babet.signal semantics while
    ensuring default termination leaves curses cleanly first.
[x] Define controlled cleanup for normal completion, explicit stop, uncaught Lua
    errors, caught pcall/binding errors, managed C++ exceptions and controlled
    termination signals without calling ncurses from a POSIX handler.
[x] Explicitly forbid ncurses calls from SIGSEGV/SIGABRT/fatal handlers and
    document `reset` as the manual fallback after an unrecoverable crash.
[x] Use `setupterm(..., &errret)` plus `newterm()` rather than `initscr()` so
    missing/unknown TERM and missing terminfo become Lua errors, never library
    exit(). Never silently substitute another TERM.
[x] Preserve one-file autonomy with normal system terminfo preferred and a small
    common `--with-fallbacks` set compiled into ncursesw; no tic/infocmp or other
    external tool is required at runtime or by --create-exe.
[x] Keep UTF-8 locale handling thread-local to the curses main thread instead of
    mutating the process-global locale; fail cleanly if no usable UTF-8 locale
    exists.
[x] Define the Lot 5 PTY/integration test matrix, including termios restoration,
    fallback terminfo, UTF-8, resize, signals, errors, interactive spawn, worker
    rejection, monitor isolation and binary-size/runtime-dependency inspection.
[x] Add `tools/test_ncurses_design_contract.sh` and run it from the normal
    preflight so the accepted design cannot silently regress before/during Lot 5.
[x] Keep runtime sources and CMake unchanged in Lot 4. The executable code is
    therefore identical to the maintainer-validated Lot 3 tree; only design,
    roadmap/changelog and preflight wiring change here.

Lot 5 — implement ncursesw
--------------------------

[x] Implement the Lua binding only after the Lot 4 contract is accepted.
[x] Keep the Lua API consistent with Babet's simple scripting philosophy: one
    screen and eight operations (`start`, `stop`, `clear`, `refresh`, `size`,
    `move`, `write`, `readKey`) with no exposed ncurses pointers/constants.
[x] Add focused structural and PTY tests for basic use, UTF-8, redraw/resize,
    keyboard timeout, repeated sessions, Lua errors, suspension/resume, terminal
    restoration, signals, workers and interactions with interactive spawn.
[x] Make the ncurses source bootstrap safe when the Babet checkout path contains
    spaces: validate Autoconf helper files and invoke bootstrap configure through
    a relative source path; build pinned ncurses 6.6 `tic`/`infocmp` first, then
    run the final fallback build from a space-free `/tmp` workspace because
    upstream `MKfallback.sh` derives an unquoted temporary terminfo path from
    `pwd`. Publish only the completed static library/headers into Babet's cache.
[x] Keep the standalone Lua OOM/RAII regression isolated after process safe-points
    gained curses hooks: link test-only curses-neutral stubs instead of pulling a
    second ncurses build into that focused helper.
[x] Syntax-check every generated curses PTY Lua fixture with `loadfile()` before
    PTY execution and use collision-safe Lua long-bracket delimiters around shell
    snippets containing `]`, so fixture syntax errors fail fast and explicitly.
[x] Serialize the timeout/resize PTY scenario deterministically: validate the
    ordinary `readKey(timeout)` timeout first, then inject SIGWINCH only after the
    `CURSES_TIMEOUT_OK` marker so resize cannot race the timeout assertion.
[x] Keep Babet as the sole SIGWINCH owner at runtime: compile ncursesw with
    its internal SIGWINCH handler disabled, use `resize_term()` rather than
    `resizeterm()` so ncurses does not enqueue its own `KEY_RESIZE`, and guard
    the optional `KEY_RESIZE` symbol. Babet alone delivers the logical resize
    event to Lua.
[x] Run the Ctrl-Z PTY regression with a shell-like parent in the same session
    and Babet in a distinct foreground process group. A direct `pty.fork()` exec
    makes Babet's group orphaned and cannot faithfully validate SIGTSTP job
    control even when Babet restores/re-enters curses correctly.
[x] Make every top-level `run_tests.sh` invocation automatically publish the
    complete color-free `babet-tests.txt` log (normal, sanitizers and release),
    while nested release stages inherit a guard and do not overwrite each other.
[x] Validate terminal ownership/restoration and autonomous packaged-curses
    behavior with `tools/test_curses_pty.sh` on the maintainer Linux machine.
    Final deterministic validation on 2026-08-24 passed every curses PTY/runtime
    scenario, including the TIOCSWINSZ-only resize path, UTF-8, workers,
    interactive spawn/stop/resume, fallback terminfo, controlled signals,
    Ctrl-Z/CONT, generated executable autonomy and absence of dynamic
    ncurses/tinfo dependencies; the complete top-level run finished with 3810/0
    folder, 3796/0 embedded, 3796/0 embedded-via-PATH and 9/9 modes OK.
[x] Measure and record the stripped binary-size delta caused by ncursesw. The
    maintainer validation binary is 15,436,616 bytes, +439,488 bytes (+2.93%)
    over the 14,997,128-byte published v2.22.2 baseline; `BINARY_SIZE.md` records
    the result without replacing the published-release baseline.
[x] Do not add an NCURSES=ON/OFF supported CMake matrix unless a real need
    exists and both configurations are tested.

Lot 6 — libbabet / embedding
----------------------------

[x] Design the first host-facing path in `EMBEDDING_DESIGN.md`: C/C++ host ->
    experimental C API -> static `libbabet.a` -> the same Babet/Lua runtime.
[x] Start static-first instead of forcing `libbabet.so`: current pinned third-
    party archives do not yet carry a documented PIC/shared-library contract,
    and the host API must be exercised before widening the build/deployment
    surface. A shared library remains a later decision, not a rejected goal.
[x] Prefer a deliberately small C boundary with an opaque `babet_context`,
    status codes, version/status access, create/run/last-error/destroy, and no
    public `lua_State`, STL type, C++ class or exception ABI.
[x] Define the initial lifecycle honestly: one live embedding context per
    process, created/used/destroyed on one host thread; simultaneous contexts
    return BUSY and wrong-thread use is rejected because signals, terminal
    ownership and Babet's main-thread identity are process-wide today.
[x] Perform only the internal split proven necessary by embedding: move the
    exact existing `register_babet()` implementation and terminal-aware Lua
    teardown out of `main.cpp` so CLI, workers and embedding share one runtime
    implementation. Do not introduce a broader runtime/CLI/builder refactor.
[x] Make CMake build runtime/project-core code as static `libbabet.a`; keep the
    official `babet` executable linked statically to that archive at build time
    so the distributed CLI remains autonomous and gains no runtime libbabet.so
    dependency. Treat this first archive as an in-tree development artifact; a
    standalone install/export SDK remains a follow-up after API validation.
[x] Add a real C smoke host (not C++) exercising Babet bindings, bundled modules,
    workers, Lua-error recovery, second-context rejection, wrong-thread use and
    sequential recreation.
[x] Add structural embedding contracts and integrate both structural and runtime
    embedding checks into `run_tests.sh`.
[x] Validate the new static-library build and C smoke host on the maintainer
    Linux machine: `libbabet.a`, exported C symbols, the real C host lifecycle,
    bundled modules, workers, error recovery and the complete existing Babet
    campaign are green; the official binary has no dynamic libbabet dependency.
[x] Keep the C API explicitly experimental during Lot 6; do not freeze its ABI
    before the embedding interface has been exercised by real use.
[x] After the first green host integration, choose explicit file/module
    search-root support as the next real-use slice before value marshalling or
    shared-library packaging.
[x] Add `babet_context_set_search_root()`: one absolute on-disk module root,
    configured before the first run, using the same `?.lua` / `?/init.lua`
    semantics as folder mode and propagated to workers. Maintainer validation
    confirms the embedding block at 6 PASS / 0 FAIL, including missing/file
    root classification, parent requires and worker propagation.
[x] Harden blocking `babet.curses.readKey()` against the deferred-signal
    lost-wakeup race observed during Lot 6: use <=50 ms internal ncurses wait
    slices with main-thread terminal/signal safe-points, preserve monotonic
    public timeout semantics and keep `readKey(0)` truly nonblocking. Maintainer
    validation now passes Ctrl-Z/CONT, the full ncurses PTY matrix and 9/9 modes.
[x] Add the smallest useful host/Lua value exchange without exposing
    `lua_State *`: a tagged scalar `babet_value` plus main-state
    `babet_context_set_global()` / `babet_context_get_global()` for nil,
    boolean, signed 64-bit integer, double and binary string. Reject structured
    Lua values explicitly and keep worker globals isolated. Maintainer
    validation confirms the scalar entry points and C smoke at 7 PASS / 0 FAIL,
    followed by the complete 9/9 Babet campaign.
[x] Add the first direct scalar call path with
    `babet_context_call_global()`: one function from the main Lua global table,
    zero or more scalar arguments, exactly one scalar result, binary-safe
    strings, protected Lua error/OOM containment and wrong-thread rejection.
    Keep structured arguments/results, callbacks, dotted methods and multi-return
    marshalling deferred. Maintainer validation confirms the exported call API,
    functional C smoke at 8 PASS / 0 FAIL and the complete 9/9 Babet campaign.
[x] Export the validated static embedding path as a relocatable normal-build
    SDK: public C header plus one flattened `libbabet.a` containing Babet and its
    pinned static third-party archives. Prove the package outside the source
    tree by moving it to a path containing spaces, compiling a fresh C host with
    only that SDK and linking through a C++ driver plus Linux system libraries.
    Keep sanitizer validation on the actual instrumented in-tree libbabet and
    do not present ASan/UBSan archives as redistributable SDKs. Maintainer
    validation on 2026-08-25 confirms the relocated out-of-tree host compiles,
    links and runs using only the standalone SDK; embedding finishes 12 PASS /
    0 FAIL and the complete normal campaign finishes 3810/0 folder, 3796/0
    embedded, 3796/0 embedded-via-PATH and 9/9 modes OK.
[x] Lot 6 decision: the first static C/C++ embedding path is complete and remains
    deliberately experimental. Do not widen it to shared-library packaging,
    callbacks, structured marshalling, multiple contexts or plugin ABI work
    without a concrete use case; move on to the optional GUI study.

Lot 7 — optional GUI study
--------------------------

[x] Study a real GUI only after embedding is stable. Lot 6 is now repeatably
    green with the standalone SDK/host path at 12 PASS / 0 FAIL and the normal
    campaign at 9/9 modes OK, so the GUI study has a real host boundary to use.
[x] Compare real GUI candidates on binary/dependency cost, deployment,
    maintenance, Linux fit, future Windows plausibility, Lua integration and
    interaction with --create-exe. `GUI_STUDY.md` reviews FLTK 1.4.x, wxWidgets
    3.2.x, GTK 4, Qt 6 and non-widget SDL/ImGui-style stacks without treating
    package-manager size as a fake final-binary measurement.
[x] Keep GUI code out of the normal `babet` executable and ordinary build. A
    future GUI is a separate companion host consuming the relocatable libbabet
    SDK; the existing CLI and --create-exe semantics gain no GUI dependency,
    plugin directory or toolkit bootstrap.
[x] Select FLTK 1.4.x as the preferred first prototype backend because it is
    cross-platform, C++, intentionally small/modular and explicitly friendly to
    static linking. Keep the modern Linux Wayland/X11 hybrid rather than forcing
    X11-only merely to trim dependencies, and measure a real stripped prototype
    before promoting FLTK to a permanent product dependency.
[x] Keep wxWidgets 3.2.x as the first fallback if native widget integration
    proves more important than minimal deployment. GTK 4 remains a strong
    Linux/GNOME option but is not the default cross-platform host; Qt 6 is
    rejected for the first Babet GUI host because its framework/deployment and
    open-source static-link obligations are disproportionate here.
[x] Define the next implementation gate without reopening generic plugin work:
    a concrete GUI host may justify one narrow host-function registration API
    over scalar `babet_value` arguments/results, integer widget IDs and named Lua
    callbacks invoked through the existing `babet_context_call_global()` path.
    Do not expose `lua_State *`, retained Lua function handles or a generic .so
    ABI merely for GUI convenience.
[x] Lot 7 decision: the optional GUI study is complete. Do not ship GUI code yet;
    if a concrete GUI use case is pursued, prototype it as a separate FLTK host
    on top of libbabet and measure its actual Linux binary/dependency closure
    before widening either Babet's embedding API or release surface.
[x] Candidate 1 maintainer validation reached the new GUI-study preflight at
    27 PASS / 0 FAIL, compiled successfully and kept packaging/embedding green,
    but exposed an older ncurses Ctrl-Z/CONT edge case: the PTY supervisor saw
    `CURSES_TSTP_READY` but never observed the Babet child in WIFSTOPPED state.
[x] Harden the deferred curses SIGTSTP path so controlled suspension no longer
    depends on the caller's implicit signal mask: queue SIGTSTP while blocked,
    switch it to SIG_DFL, explicitly unblock it to stop, then re-block briefly,
    restore Babet's handlers and restore the exact previous thread signal mask
    after SIGCONT. Extend the ncurses structural preflight to protect this mask
    lifecycle; the focused structural block now passes 70 PASS / 0 FAIL.
[x] Candidate 2 maintainer validation closes Lot 7: GUI-study preflight 27
    PASS / 0 FAIL, hardened ncurses structural contracts 70 PASS / 0 FAIL,
    real Ctrl-Z/CONT PTY scenario PASS, 3810/0 folder, 3796/0 embedded,
    3796/0 embedded-via-PATH and the complete normal campaign 9/9 modes OK.

Lot 8 — developer documentation for libbabet
---------------------------------------------

[x] Turn the Lot 6 design contract into a practical bilingual developer guide
    without pretending the experimental ABI is already frozen.
[x] Document the complete current lifecycle: create, optional search root, run,
    scalar globals, direct scalar global calls, diagnostics and destroy.
[x] Document the exact supported scalar types, binary-safe string input and the
    borrowed lifetime of strings returned by get_global()/call_global().
[x] Document BUSY, WRONG_THREAD, process-wide side effects, recovery after a Lua
    error, C++ host use through the C ABI, and all deliberately deferred surface.
[x] Add six small standalone C examples for minimal lifecycle, search root,
    scalar values/binary strings, direct calls, error recovery and thread/context
    lifecycle, plus a CMake project that builds them against the standalone SDK.
[x] Make the standalone SDK publish both developer guides and the executable
    examples instead of requiring users to return to the Babet source tree.
[x] Extend embedding structural/runtime regressions so the moved SDK examples are
    configured, compiled and executed out of tree.
[x] Document Linux/glibc compatibility correctly: GNU/Linux 3.2.0 ELF metadata
    is not a glibc baseline; measure versioned GLIBC_* requirements with objdump,
    and do not promise a fixed glibc minimum for arbitrary SDK-linked hosts.
[x] Make the embedding runtime regression report the highest measured GLIBC_*
    requirement of the maintained Babet binary and freshly linked external host
    when objdump is available.
[x] Lot 8 design result: writing real examples did not require widening or
    correcting the public embedding runtime API; keep the API experimental and
    proceed only after complete maintainer validation.
[x] Final maintainer validation on 2026-08-25 closes Lot 8: embedding structural
    contracts 199 PASS / 0 FAIL, SDK builder regression 7 PASS / 0 FAIL, moved
    SDK examples build and execute 6/6, embedding runtime regression 14 PASS /
    0 FAIL, measured highest GLIBC_* requirement GLIBC_2.38 for both the rebuilt
    maintained Babet binary and freshly linked SDK host, and the complete normal
    campaign remains green at 3810/0 folder, 3796/0 embedded, 3796/0 embedded
    via PATH and 9/9 modes OK.

Lot 9 — separate FLTK prototype
-------------------------------

[x] Build a deliberately tiny FLTK 1.4.5 companion host on the standalone
    libbabet SDK; add no FLTK dependency to the normal Babet CLI/build.
[x] Keep the first prototype intentionally limited to one window, one button and
    one counter/state transition, using existing call_global() for host -> Lua.
[x] Do not hide the missing Lua -> host direction with polling, shared globals or
    other workarounds. `FLTK_PROTOTYPE.md` records the concrete missing-capability
    list that becomes the Lot 10 requirements.
[x] Define and implement callback failure policy: a deliberate Lua failure on the
    second callback is logged/contained by the host, `Fl::run()` continues, and
    the third callback must recover successfully.
[x] Make the C++ FLTK callback itself a no-throw boundary so an unexpected host
    exception is contained before returning to the toolkit event loop.
[x] Define and test destruction ordering in the prototype: disable callbacks,
    detach/destroy FLTK objects retaining host state, then destroy babet_context;
    callback guards remain safe after context destruction.
[x] Keep every FLTK/widget operation on the GUI/main thread. Record Fl::awake()
    notification as the future worker-to-GUI direction instead of allowing
    direct worker widget manipulation.
[x] Document that GUI callbacks run on the event-loop thread and should stay
    short; long/blocking work must be moved away from that loop.
[x] Pin the optional prototype bootstrap to FLTK 1.4.5 with SHA-256 verification.
    The normal build and normal run_tests never download or configure FLTK.
[x] Add a separate self-test that drives real FLTK callbacks through Fl::run(),
    verifies Lua-error recovery, measures the stripped prototype and prints its
    ldd closure while rejecting a dynamic libfltk runtime.
[x] Run the complete normal Babet campaign plus `./prototypes/fltk/test.sh` on the
    maintainer Linux desktop: normal Babet remains 9/9 modes OK; the FLTK
    self-test recovers after the intentional Lua callback failure; the stripped
    prototype measures 15,288,072 bytes and links FLTK statically while its
    measured dynamic closure is the expected system X11/C/C++ runtime stack.
[x] Re-run the optional FLTK validation after adding its dedicated complete
    `babet-fltk-tests.txt` transcript: maintainer validation on 2026-08-25
    confirms the separate transcript is published correctly, the event-loop
    self-test remains green, static FLTK has no dynamic runtime dependency,
    and the normal Babet CLI still has zero GUI runtime dependency.

Inter-lot maintenance — complete local cleanup
----------------------------------------------

[x] Keep the default `clear_code.sh` mode focused on fast rebuild artefacts:
    `build/`, `test/` and the legacy `src/third_party/` tree.
[x] Make `clear_code.sh --all` a real reset of known generated project state by
    additionally removing `downloads/`, `dist/`, `babet-tests.txt`,
    `babet-fltk-tests.txt`, `MODIFIED_FILES.txt` and `GITHUB_RELEASE_*.md`.
[x] Never implement the full reset with broad archive/document globs: unrelated
    user ZIP/Markdown files at the project root must survive `--all`.
[x] Add an isolated cleanup regression and run it from the normal preflight.

Lot 10 — narrow Lua -> host C/C++ function API
-----------------------------------------------

[x] Design the smallest host-function registration surface from the concrete
    missing-capability list produced by the FLTK prototype, not from speculation.
[x] Keep the public boundary C-only and reuse babet_value scalar arguments/results
    first; expose no lua_State, STL types, C++ classes or exception ABI.
[x] Define callback lifetime, name ownership, error conversion, worker isolation,
    reentrancy and owner-thread rules explicitly before implementation.
[x] Publish registered functions only as `babet.host.<name>` with copied simple
    identifier names excluding Lua reserved keywords, host-owned userdata lasting
    until context destruction and no
    unregister operation in this first slice.
[x] Use the opaque `babet_host_call` accessors/setters so scalar result strings and
    callback diagnostics are copied by Babet before temporary host storage dies.
[x] Reject nested `babet_context_*` entry from an active callback with the explicit
    `BABET_STATUS_REENTRANT_CALL` status; workers do not inherit host callbacks.
[x] Rework the Lot 9 prototype onto the public host-function API and remove every
    temporary/prototype-only bridge rather than keeping a workaround because it
    happens to work.
[x] Add C host regressions for scalar/binary arguments, host errors, recovery,
    reentrancy, late/wrong-thread registration and worker isolation, plus a C++
    regression proving thrown exceptions are contained and the context recovers.
[x] Ship the host-function design contract and a standalone SDK example, and extend
    the bilingual embedding documentation before treating this API as the value-
    passage foundation for the future native-plugin experiment.
[x] Run the complete maintainer `./run_tests.sh` campaign and the separate
    `./prototypes/fltk/test.sh` runtime validation on Lot 10 Candidate 2. Final
    maintainer validation on 2026-08-25 is green: host-function contracts reach
    52 PASS / 0 FAIL, embedding runtime reaches 16 PASS / 0 FAIL, all seven moved
    SDK examples pass, the normal campaign remains 3810/0 folder, 3796/0 embedded,
    3796/0 embedded via PATH and 9/9 modes, and the FLTK self-test proves the full
    Lua -> host round trip with `host_updates=2`. The stripped FLTK companion measures
    15,316,744 bytes, keeps FLTK static, and the normal Babet CLI retains zero GUI
    runtime dependency.

Lot 11 — minimal native C/C++ plugin experiment
-----------------------------------------------

[x] Reopen native plugins for the real extension use cases: libraries that
    Babet should never absorb into core (for example specialised/vendor SDKs)
    and third-party/private extensions that should not require forking Babet.
    Binary-size reduction is explicitly NOT a plugin architecture motivation.
[x] Define a tiny versioned C plugin entry point/declaration ABI. Plugin internals
    may use C++, but no C++ exception, RTTI/STL object or allocator ownership may
    cross the Babet/plugin boundary because the host/plugin runtime configuration
    is not under Babet's control. ABI v1 is a static descriptor returned by
    `babet_plugin_query_v1()` and a bounded list of `babet_host_function`.
[x] Keep the first plugin ABI away from lua_State and reuse the Lot 10 scalar
    host-function mechanism for value passage instead of exposing Lua internals.
[x] Treat native plugins as fully trusted in-process code with no sandbox or
    memory-safety boundary.
[x] Load explicitly chosen .so files with RTLD_NOW|RTLD_LOCAL and keep successful
    plugins loaded for process lifetime; do not support unload/reload while
    callbacks/functions may remain reachable.
[x] Refuse native-plugin loading from generated --create-exe applications in the
    first design and do not weaken the one-file generated-application contract.
    Lot 11 also refuses loading from workers and external embedding hosts.
[x] Do not add a package manager, dependency resolver, Internet downloader,
    automatic require() discovery, /tmp extraction or plugin packaging until a
    later concrete need proves that complexity worthwhile.
[x] Run the complete maintainer campaign on Linux (`./run_tests.sh`) plus the
    separate FLTK regression (`./prototypes/fltk/test.sh`) because Lot 11
    generalises the public `babet_host_call_*` implementation reused by Lot 10.
    Candidate 1 reached the native plugin runtime and exposed only an unquoted
    Babet executable path in its shell regression on a checkout path containing
    spaces; Candidate 2 quotes that invocation and protects it structurally.
    Final maintainer validation on 2026-08-25 is green: native plugin structural
    contracts 74/0, native plugin runtime 4/0, embedding runtime 16/0, packaging
    8/0, folder tests 3810/0, embedded tests 3796/0, embedded-via-PATH 3796/0 and
    9/9 modes. The stripped CLI measures 15,510,344 bytes (+513,216 bytes versus
    the published v2.22.2 baseline). The separate FLTK regression still proves
    the Lot 10 Lua -> host round trip with `host_updates=2`, measures 15,337,224
    bytes stripped, keeps FLTK static and leaves the normal CLI with zero GUI
    runtime dependency.

Post-audit hardening — Lots 10/11
----------------------------------

[x] Reopen Lots 10/11 after the independent 2026-08-25 code audit instead of
    publishing the first green implementation unchanged. Keep commit 90155eb as
    the pre-audit checkpoint and apply hardening as a separate follow-up.
[x] Make the plugin callback type distinct from embedding `babet_host_function`
    and `noexcept` in C++; C++ plugins must catch their own exceptions because the
    official statically-linked Babet C++ runtime cannot reliably catch across an
    independently linked plugin DSO. Add a compile-fail regression and an
    official-runtime caught-exception/recovery scenario.
[x] Make host callbacks, plugin functions and `babet.plugin.load()` coroutine-safe
    by validating runtime identity through the Lua registry shared by coroutines,
    not by comparing the current `lua_State *` to the main thread pointer.
[x] Harden the experimental plugin descriptor while it is still unpublished:
    length-delimited metadata/function names avoid unbounded NUL scans, and an
    explicit `function_struct_size` byte stride permits forward-compatible tails.
[x] Reject duplicate shared objects by canonical/underlying-file identity and by
    `dlopen()` handle identity, covering hardlinks and alternate DSO paths.
[x] Make embedding host-function publication use raw Lua table operations and
    reserve/commit ownership so user `__index`/`__newindex` metamethods cannot
    observe a half-installed closure or retain a dangling registration.
[x] Probe `--export-dynamic-symbol` support during CMake configuration and fail
    with an explicit capability diagnostic on an unsupported linker.
[x] Accept explicit Linux `.so` and `.so.<version>` filenames, remove the unused
    internal `result_set` flag, update C/C++ examples, and document failed-probe
    `dlclose()` constructor constraints.
[x] Run the complete maintainer `./run_tests.sh` and separate
    `./prototypes/fltk/test.sh` campaigns on the hardened candidate. Final
    post-audit validation on 2026-08-25 is green: host-function contracts 58/0,
    native-plugin structural contracts 98/0, embedding structural contracts
    220/0, native-plugin runtime 5/0, embedding runtime 16/0, folder tests
    3810/0, embedded tests 3796/0, embedded-via-PATH 3796/0 and 9/9 modes. The
    stripped CLI measures 15,522,632 bytes (+525,504 bytes versus the published
    v2.22.2 baseline). The FLTK regression remains green with `host_updates=2`,
    measures 15,345,416 bytes stripped, keeps FLTK static and leaves the normal
    CLI with zero GUI runtime dependency.
[x] Request a second independent audit of the hardened source before any GitHub
    push/release. The second audit on 2026-08-25 revalidated all six original
    findings as corrected, confirmed the strengthened ABI/runtime regressions,
    and concluded the code is ready to publish. Three final contract nits were
    closed afterwards without architectural change: numeric-only `.so.<version>`
    suffixes, `reserved == 0`, and local C++ callback symbols in the examples.
[x] Run one final maintainer `./run_tests.sh` and separate FLTK regression after
    those three second-audit contract nits. Final post-audit validation on
    2026-08-25 is green: host-function contracts 58/0, native-plugin structural
    contracts 108/0, embedding structural contracts 220/0, native-plugin runtime
    6/0, embedding runtime 16/0, folder tests 3810/0, embedded tests 3796/0,
    embedded-via-PATH 3796/0 and 9/9 modes. The stripped CLI measures 15,522,632
    bytes (+525,504 bytes versus the published v2.22.2 baseline). The separate
    FLTK regression remains green with `host_updates=2`, measures 15,349,512
    bytes stripped, keeps FLTK static and leaves the normal CLI with zero GUI
    runtime dependency. No further external audit is required unless later code
    changes reopen this surface.
[x] Close the fixed-width ABI tag maintainability gap found during release review:
    freeze every existing `babet_status` / `babet_value_type` numeric assignment
    with compile-time assertions, document the intentional loss of C++ type
    separation/`-Wswitch`, and make the embedding structural preflight derive the
    public tag sets from `babet.h` and compare them against every exhaustive
    validator, symbolic-name mapper and scalar converter. Future tag additions
    must update all relevant handlers before release validation can pass.
[x] Re-run `./run_tests.sh --release` and `./prototypes/fltk/test.sh` after the
    final ABI-tag coverage guard. Final 2026-08-25 validation is green:
    embedding structural contracts 230/0, host-function contracts 58/0,
    native-plugin structural contracts 108/0, ASan/UBSan embedding runtime
    10/0, normal embedding runtime 16/0, native-plugin runtime 6/0, folder
    3810/0, embedded 3796/0, embedded-via-PATH 3796/0, 9/9 top-level modes,
    network smoke tests 11/0/0 WARN and the separate FLTK event-loop regression
    green at 15,349,512 stripped bytes with no dynamic FLTK dependency.

Deferred Windows port
---------------------

[ ] Keep Linux as the reference platform until --create-exe, ncurses, embedding,
    optional GUI decisions, tests, and documentation are mature.
[ ] Then study babet.exe/libbabet.dll and Windows-specific process, signal,
    terminal/PTY, path, filesystem, dynamic-library, and build differences.
[ ] Never combine the Windows port with a simultaneous major Linux refactor.

Per-lot delivery discipline
---------------------------

[ ] For every future modified lot: inspect current state, explain the minimal
    change, modify only what is needed, add/adapt tests, reach green, update this
    TODO, update docs/changelog when relevant, and deliver two ZIP archives:
    complete project + modified/added files only.


Completed historical milestones (reference)
============================================

Babet 2.9.x roadmap
====================

[x] Lot A: add configurable babet.spawn() redirections.
[x] Preserve the historical three-pipe default without breaking existing code.
[x] Add inherit, /dev/null, regular-file output and stderr-to-stdout merging.
[x] Add exhaustive validation, lifecycle compatibility tests and rich FR/EN/PDF documentation.
[x] Lot B: add binary-safe native babet.base64.encode()/decode().
[x] Lot C: add generic secure babet.writeFileAtomic().
[x] Lot D: harden worker lifecycle with strict options, status(), join(timeout)
    and cooperative cancellation.
[x] Lot E: add bounded direct worker-to-worker channels after lifecycle rules
    and shared transport primitives are stable.
[x] Perform the final code/tests/docs/PDF/build/release audit.

Babet 2.9.1 corrective release
------------------------------

[x] Fix chunked and connection-close HTTP response framing with cpp-httplib
    0.45.0 while preserving Babet-owned body/file limits.
[x] Add exact-boundary, fragmented, truncated, download-cleanup and
    close-delimited regression tests.
[x] Add independent blocking local smoke tests for both response receivers:
    HTTPS for chunked framing and HTTP for connection-close framing.

Babet 2.9.2 hardening release
-----------------------------

[x] Harden worker serialization with node and byte budgets, exact integer
    fidelity and exception-safe Lua boundaries.
[x] Harden spawn/exec/pipeline/spawnPipeline cleanup against C++ exceptions and
    post-fork allocation failures.
[x] Harden SQLite transaction recovery across callback, COMMIT and ROLLBACK
    failures, with reusable connection-state tests.
[x] Harden HTTP, socket and TLS exception boundaries and verify that
    Authorization is stripped on cross-origin redirects.
[x] Extend deterministic Zstandard, inotify, worker-budget and network
    regression coverage; validate the exact release tree under ASan + UBSan and
    the final normal build.

Babet 2.10.0 SQLite audit release
---------------------------------

[x] Audit every SQLite Lua entry point, native handle, bind contract and
    deferred-close path.
[x] Add the bind-only babet.sqlite.NULL sentinel without changing SQL NULL
    reads.
[x] Preserve useful step diagnostics after the parent connection userdata is
    collected and prove the zombie-connection regressions.
[x] Synchronise strict tests, French/English documentation and release
    metadata.

Babet 2.11.0 bounded archive-read release
-----------------------------------------

[x] Add babet.archive.read() for exact raw-name or one-based index selection.
[x] Refuse ambiguous names while allowing explicit duplicate disambiguation by
    index.
[x] Bound both announced and actually produced bytes with opts.max_size.
[x] Cover ZIP, plain/gzip/xz/bzip2/zstd TAR, raw names, workers, strict options,
    integrity errors and zero filesystem writes.
[x] Synchronise rich French/English documentation and release metadata.

Babet 2.12.0 SQLite connection conveniences
--------------------------------------------

[x] Add db:last_insert_rowid(), db:changes() and db:total_changes() with
    strict arity, closed-handle errors and 64-bit SQLite values.
[x] Add opts.readonly without changing the default READWRITE | CREATE mode.
[x] Add per-connection opts.foreign_keys with an explicit false default.
[x] Reject readonly=true with wal=true and cover all compatible combinations.
[x] Verify the new surface in the main Lua state and in workers.
[x] Cover ignored INSERT rowids and deferred foreign-key COMMIT failure.
[x] Apply the archive exception boundary to all public archive functions and
    verify TAR in-memory read completion explicitly.
[x] Synchronise rich French/English documentation and release metadata.

Babet 2.13.0 nested savepoint helper
------------------------------------

[x] Add db:savepoint(callback) with generated internal identifiers.
[x] Support standalone, managed-transaction, manual-transaction and nested
    savepoint scopes.
[x] Roll back callback failures with ROLLBACK TO followed by RELEASE.
[x] Clean up failed outermost RELEASE operations without forwarding callback
    results.
[x] Refuse db:close() while any savepoint callback is active.
[x] Cover strict arity, closed handles, nested recovery, deferred constraints,
    connection reuse and worker availability.
[x] Synchronise rich French/English documentation and release metadata.

Babet 2.14.0 remaining Lua/C++ exception boundaries
---------------------------------------------------

[x] Add one allocation-free exception-classification core shared by public
    Lua C function boundaries.
[x] Protect both compression functions and preserve atomic-output cleanup.
[x] Protect the six flat SYS functions without changing ordinary returns.
[x] Protect user.get and user.exists around their C++/NSS allocations.
[x] Protect the inotify factory and methods, with a non-throwing __gc wrapper.
[x] Add a compiled four-path self-test, verify reporting after catch cleanup,
    and reject all 17 direct registrations.
[x] Add PTY coverage for Ctrl+Z cleanup and terminal restoration after an
    is_running() status refresh.
[x] Document the current job-control boundary and the non-interactive pipeline
    contract in French and English.
[x] Synchronise French/English documentation and release metadata.

Babet 2.15.0 Lua longjmp / C++ RAII audit

[x] Inventory allocating Lua API calls made while binding-owned C++ RAII
    objects are alive.
[x] Audit local exception handling in socket, SQLite, archive and the remaining
    public modules before deciding whether any common-boundary migration is
    appropriate.
[x] Add focused tests before changing cleanup or boundary structure.
[x] Close the remaining process/pipeline registrations, structured userdata
    construction, and resource-owning finalizers identified by the final
    external audit.

Babet 2.16.0 process terminal lifecycle and limited job control

[x] Inventory stopped/continued child states and preserve the existing final
    result table while observing WUNTRACED/WCONTINUED.
[x] Add process:state() with running/stopped/exited/closed states.
[x] Make wait() report a stopped child promptly and keep is_running() true
    until final exit.
[x] Add process:resume([foreground]) with saved child termios and foreground
    restoration for interactive children.
[x] Reclaim the terminal asynchronously after direct-child exit without Lua
    work or terminal cleanup in a SIGCHLD handler.
[x] Add red PTY tests for stop/resume and exit-before-parent-query, then make
    both scenarios pass.
[x] Serialize terminal handoffs and add a deterministic regression for two
    immediate interactive spawns while the first exit monitor is delayed.
[x] Keep pipeline()/spawnPipeline() non-interactive.
[x] Synchronise French/English documentation, changelogs, release notes and
    PDF manuals.

Babet 2.17.0 process launch hardening
-----------------------------------------

[x] Upgrade the embedded runtime to Lua 5.5.1.
[x] Prepare the effective environment, PATH candidates, argv and envp in the
    parent, then use execve only after fork.
[x] Preserve ENOENT/ENOTDIR/EACCES lookup precedence and document the explicit
    ENOEXEC no-shell migration.
[x] Bound interactive terminal reservations and split launch/terminal internals.
[x] Close the remaining raw Lua table-access and PreparedCommand lifetime risks.

Babet 2.18.0 persistent bounded worker pool
--------------------------------------------

[x] Add workers.cpu_count() and non-consuming job:done().
[x] Add a fixed reusable worker pool with bounded queue and outstanding count.
[x] Add task handles, monotonic timeouts, FIFO close, retryable join and
    cooperative whole-pool cancellation.
[x] Isolate ordinary task globals while reusing each persistent Lua state and
    its package.loaded cache.
[x] Support explicitly shared channels and keep forced task termination out.
[x] Synchronise tests, French/English documentation, release notes and PDFs.

Possible later work retained outside 2.18:
- native SQLite backup through sqlite3_backup_*;
- small Linux-only additions such as find(..., {xdev = true}), Unix sockets,
  or narrowly justified signal helpers;
- forced worker termination remains intentionally excluded without a safe
  cooperative design.

No macOS or BSD port is planned: Babet remains Linux-only.
